**Assumption 1.3 (finite globally feasible dual and joint attainment).**
Supply finite $t\ge\max(t_0,30000)$, finite $\lambda>0$ and
$y\in\mathbb R^8$, and a finite common law $\pi$ in (RB.2), such that

$$
\begin{gathered}
y\cdot\Gamma(x,\xi)\ge|\ell(x)+tc(x)\xi f(x)|+\lambda|u|
\quad((x,\xi)\in X),\\
J_t(\pi)+\lambda K(\pi)=y\cdot B(t)=C_t(\lambda).
\end{gathered}
\tag{RB.3}
$$

Here $C_t$ is the same common-law maximum in GC.74 and FC.4.
No existence of such finite duals is assumed outside the supplied
instance. In particular this assumption supplies neither $\lambda=0$
data, nonfinite or unattained duals, unsupplied boundary attainment,
nor an infinite-budget instrument.

Write the dual polynomials and matrices in the FC.27 normalization:

$$
\begin{gathered}
P=P_y=y_0+y_1u+y_2v+y_3u^2+y_4uv,\qquad
Q=\begin{pmatrix}y_5+y_6&y_7/2\\y_7/2&y_5\end{pmatrix},\\
R_-=Q+t\mathsf F,\qquad R_+=Q-t\mathsf F.
\end{gathered}
\tag{RB.4}
$$

Thus $y\cdot\Gamma=P+\xi c\,x^TQx$. The global pure and saturated
signed tests, after division by their positive denominators, are
$P-\epsilon\lambda u-\sigma\ell$ and
$P-\epsilon\lambda u-\sigma\ell+c\,x^T(Q-\sigma t\mathsf F)x$,
for all $\epsilon,\sigma\in\{\pm1\}$ on the entire directed circle.
These are FC.7 and RC.70, with
$\lambda_{\rm RC}=\sqrt\alpha\,\lambda$.

